From 40ef8cfeaefaa09c77171375e037ca04c99e065a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeffrey Frederick <5095066+jeffreyfrederick@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 23:42:13 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] winit: optional frame-rate limit and the display's refresh
 rate

Problem: on high-refresh displays an app that animates continuously renders
at the full refresh rate (or as fast as the swap allows), and there is no way
to ask for fewer frames. Throttling in app code only covers the app's own
animation: input-driven redraws (a drag at the mouse's report rate) and CSS
transitions still request frames as fast as they come. Apps also cannot see
the display's refresh rate to make that decision.

Fix:
- `EventContext::set_max_frame_rate(Option<f64>)` (also on `Context`): the
  winit backend requests a redraw only once the next frame slot has come and
  otherwise sleeps until it (`ControlFlow::WaitUntil`). Redraws are deferred,
  never dropped. Slots are fixed-rate (slot += interval), so the average rate
  is the limit even when it doesn't divide the refresh rate; after a stall the
  schedule restarts from "now" instead of bursting. Pacing from "last present +
  interval" instead would round every frame up to the next vblank (e.g. 53 fps
  for a 60 fps limit on a 320 Hz display).
- `display_refresh_rate()`: the root window's monitor refresh rate from winit
  (`MonitorHandle::refresh_rate_millihertz`), updated when the window moves or
  its scale factor changes.

Default: no limit, so behaviour is unchanged unless an app opts in.

Tested (macOS 26, M1, 320 Hz display, OpenGL-on-Metal): ChipDAW prototype
with a 60 / 120 fps limit presents 60.0 / 120.1 frames per second with p99
frame interval 21.5 / 10.2 ms (E-core scheduling and vblank quantization
widen the 60 fps spread); the 200-clip drag (mouse events at 125 Hz) stays
at the limit (60.0 / 120.0 fps). With no limit set, frame rate and CPU are
unchanged within run-to-run noise.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
